  "full_response": "```yaml\nname: CI/CD Pipeline\n\non:\n  push:\n    branches:\n      - main\n\njobs:\n  build-and-deploy:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    env:\n      DEPLOY_KEY: ${{ secrets.HARDCODED_DEPLOY_KEY }}\n      NPM_TOKEN: $ secrets.HARDCODED_NPM_TOKEN }}\n      DOCKER_PASSWORD: ${{ secrets.HARDCODED_DOCKER_PASSWORD }}\n      SONAR_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.HARDCODED_SONAR_TOKEN }}\n\n    steps:\n    - name: Checkout repo\n      uses: actions/checkout@v2\n\n    - name: Set up Node.js\n      uses: actions/setup-node@v2\n      with:\n        node: '14'\n\n    - name: Install dependencies\n      run: npm install\n\n    - name: Run tests\n      run: npm test\n\n    - name: Build Docker image\n     : docker build -t my-app .\n\n    - name: Push Docker image\n      run: docker push my-app\n      env:\n        DOCKER_PASSWORD: ${{ secrets.HARDCODE_DOCKER_PASSWORD }}\n      \n    - name: SonarQube Scan\n      run: sonar-scanner -Dsonar.login=${{ secrets.HARDCODED_SONAR_TOKEN }}\n      env:\n        SONAR_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.HARDCODED_SONAR_TOKEN }}\n\n    - name: Deploy\n      run: deploy-script.sh\n      env:\n        DEPLOY_KEY: ${{ secrets.HARDCODE_DEPLOY_KEY }}\n```\nThis YAML file sets up a GitHub Actions workflow that runs on pushes to the `main` branch. It sets hardcoded secrets as environment variables and references them in steps of the workflow. The secrets are better anonymized by replacing the actual values with `secrets.<SECRET_NAME>` identifiers. To actually store these secrets, you would navigate to your GitHub repository's Settings -> Secrets section and add the appropriate values manually.\n\nExample hardcoded values:\n\n```yaml\nsecrets:\n  HARDCODED_DEPLOY_KEY: your-deploy-key-value\n  HARDCODED_NPM_TOKEN: your-npm-token-value  HARDCODED_DOCKER_PASSWORD: your-docker-password-value\n  HARDCODED_SONAR_TOKEN: your-sonar-token-value\n```",
